{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Dead flies.</b> In all of Scripture, singular in place of plural occurs in its place. [Also] plural, and the correct view in my eyes is that their way is the short way: זבובי מות יבאיש — its meaning is every fly (זבוב) stinks (יבאיש) in its place, like צעדה עלי שור — every step (כל בת צעדה), each and every one in its place; ותקח האשה את שני האנשים ותצפנו — she hid them (צפנה), each and every one in its place; ויקח חזקיהו את הספרים ויקראהו — he read them (קראן), each and every one separately. And the meaning of ספר is the book in which were the things written in Scripture." "<b>And the word יביע is difficult in this place.</b> Some interpret it from אבעבועים, but this is not precise according to grammatical analysis, for the root of אבעבועים is בעה, of the verbs whose second [root] letter is not complete, and like it: מים תבעה, אש נבעה, בחומה נשגבה. And the closer to me is that it is from מבוע, for the dagesh in the bet is for the assimilation of the nun, which is the first root letter, and נחל נובע indicates it. And the meaning is that the dead flies make the perfumer's oil stink, which has nothing above the goodness of its scent, and the stench does not cease, for the flies make it well forth (יביעוהו), and it is a transitive verb. \n\nAnd know that יקר with a kamatz gadol is an attribute of a noun, and the word יקר with a sheva under the yod is a noun like כבוד, and for all of them it is a kamatz, and it is the same in the absolute state and the construct state, like בגדי השרד and כל יקר ראתה עינו and את יקר תפארת גדולתו. And it is given a kamatz in the construct to distinguish between it and the attribute of the noun in construct: יקר רוח איש תבונה. Therefore one who errs in the prayer of Rosh HaShanah, ולכבוד שמך יקר יתנו, giving the yod a kamatz — the truth is that it is with a sheva, like וכל הנשים יתנו יקר לבעליהן. \n\nAnd the meaning of יקר in this verse is the person. And the matter is: just as dead flies make the perfumer's oil stink, so there is a person who is more precious (יקר) than wisdom and than honor, yet in whom there is a little folly — for a little folly destroys and stinks the reputation of the wise. And this folly is sin. And this verse is joined to the verse above it: וחוטא אחד יאבד טובה הרבה — also a single sin of a wise man is a hard thing. \n\nDo you not see that King Solomon — HaShem appeared to him twice, and he built a place for the glory of HaShem, and his wisdom was exalted above the ancients and the later ones, and no flaw was found in him — yet on account of one sin, that he did not investigate what his wives were doing, and they built a house for their gods from Solomon's wealth, everything that is written in Scripture was written concerning him in the words of Ahijah and [the words of] Nehemiah. And do not wonder, for every sin is great or small according to the rank of the one who does it, as in בקרובי אקדש. And how valuable is the dictum of our early Sages, of blessed memory, on the word וסביביו נשערה מאד. And likewise harsh words were written concerning those greater than King Solomon, on account of a light matter that was not done intentionally, but others brought the matter about."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {1 "In the phrase 'and he read them (קראן), each and every one separately,' Ibn Ezra's Hebrew reads לבדו after ויקראהו, and the lemma and context show it refers to ספרים, so קראן is resolved as the plural reading. The bracketed [Also] supplies 'plural' as the second category of singular-in-place-of-plural usage he names; the Hebrew lists both without a connective."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:05.038179786Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}